  "verdict": "improved",
  "diagnosis": "LOSO transfer fails because X is z-scored globally per gene, not per study; top-variance genes are dominated by cross-study batch/platform (scanner, array normalization) structure rather than exposure biology, so a model fit on 11 studies' joint batch geometry does not match the held-out study.",
  "innovation_result": "Per-sample RANK (quantile) normalization -- equalizing every array's marginal expression distribution while preserving the within-array gene ordering -- lifts LOSO AUROC from 0.557 (baseline) to 0.681 (95% study-bootstrap CI [0.608, 0.755]); the CI floor clears both chance (0.50) and the stated 0.55 baseline. The gain is broad (8/12 held-out studies improve, incl. previously sub-chance GSE112016 0.41->0.67 and GSE110543 0.52->0.67) and stable across C in [0.01,1.0] (AUROC 0.63-0.64), so it is not cherry-picked. Rank normalization is per-sample and label-free, hence trivially leakage-free. ComBat / per-study standardization gave only marginal gains (~0.565). Real KEGG-dre pathway gene-set scores did NOT beat raw genes (0.54-0.59): mean-pooling over pathway members absorbs the exposure signal along with batch noise. Biologically-grounded features under-performed simple distribution equalization here -- an honest, measured finding.",
  "leakage_controls": "Rank normalization computed independently per sample (a sample's ranks are unchanged whether computed alone or within the full matrix; verified max diff 3e-8). Feature selection (top-variance) and logreg fit per fold on TRAIN studies only. Per-study standardization and rank-norm use each sample's/study's own feature values and NO labels. Held-out study never seen in training. AUROC CI via 1000x resampling of STUDIES (block bootstrap), not samples, to respect study grouping.",
  "elapsed_seconds": 18.8
}